title: One History, Many Witnesses – The Old Testament Retold Throughout Scripture
description: Four retellings of Israel’s history, Nehemiah’s confession, Stephen’s accusation, Paul’s repetition and the redemption of Hebrews 11, read in order as one arc.
output: lessons/bible/ot-in-light-of-nt-stephen.html

# Converted from the hand-made page lessons/bible/ot-in-light-of-nt-stephen.html (11 slides, same order).
# Its slides were built by JavaScript from a SLIDES array; the content below follows that array in order.

=== Premise
--- One History, Many Witnesses [title]
overline: The Old Testament Retold Throughout Scripture
heading: One History, Many Witnesses
lead: The Old Testament’s narrative, God’s covenant with Abraham, the Exodus, the wilderness, the kings and the prophets, is not told once and set aside. It is retold again and again across Scripture, always to teach the grace and mercy of God, His longsuffering towards His people, and the tendency of those people to be prone to wander, to become puffed up, to harden their hearts and stiffen their necks, and to disobey God and provoke Him.
scripture: 1 Corinthians 10:11 | mark: they are written for our admonition
lead: This lesson follows **four** of those retellings, chosen because, read in order, they trace a single arc: confession, accusation, repetition, and redemption.
step: I. Confession | Nehemiah 9
step: II. Accusation | Acts 7, Stephen
step: III. Repetition | Acts 13, Paul
step: IV. Redemption | Hebrews 11

--- The Pattern in Scripture
lead: This is not unique to these four. Retelling Israel’s history is one of Scripture’s standard tools, used by psalmists, prophets, and apostles alike to call God’s people back to Him.
panel: Corporate Confession | Psalm 78; Psalm 105 and 106; Psalm 135 and 136: the history sung and confessed in worship.
panel: Prophetic Confrontation | Ezekiel 20; Joshua 24; 1 Samuel 12: the history used as an indictment, or a charge to choose.
panel: New Testament Theology | Galatians 3–4; Romans 9–11: the history used by Paul to explain the Law, faith, and the difference between unbelieving Israel and the believing.
take: We will look closely at just four, chosen because they trace one arc from confession to redemption.

=== I. Confession
--- Nehemiah’s Confession
kicker: I. Confession
lead: Generations before Stephen ever stood trial, the returned remnant stands in Jerusalem, reads the Law aloud, and confesses this same history as their own failure, verse for verse.
step: The Exodus and the Wilderness | God chose Abraham, delivered Israel from Egypt with signs and wonders, and led them by cloud and fire to Sinai, giving them the Law, manna, and water. [[Nehemiah 9:6-15]]
step: The Golden Calf and God’s Mercy | They hardened their necks and made a molten calf, yet God’s mercy did not depart from them, and He brought them in to possess the land. [[Nehemiah 9:16-25]]
step: The Cycle of Rebellion | In the land, the pattern repeats for generations: they rebel, God delivers them to their enemies, they cry out, and He hears and saves them again. [[Nehemiah 9:26-31]]
step: The Confession | The prayer ends by justifying God and condemning themselves. [[Nehemiah 9:32-37]]
scripture: Nehemiah 9:33 | mark: thou hast done right, but we have done wickedly

=== II. Accusation
--- Stephen’s Accusation: Abraham to Moses
kicker: II. Accusation
lead: Brought before the council of Jews in Jerusalem, Stephen uses the very history Nehemiah’s generation confessed as an accusation against his accusers. Indicting them for their rejection of God’s Messiah, Christ Jesus.
step: Abraham | Called out of his own country to a land and an inheritance he never lived to receive. [[Acts 7:2-8]]
step: Joseph | Sold by his own brothers in envy, yet God was with him, and made him ruler over Egypt. [[Acts 7:9-16]]
step: Moses | Rejected the first time he tried to deliver his people on his own, but forty years later, was chosen by God to lead them from captivity. [[Acts 7:17-34]]
fig: Genesis 12:1–3 | Genesis 12:1-3 | mark: Get thee out of thy country
fig: Exodus 3:5, 7, 10 | Exodus 3:5; Exodus 3:7; Exodus 3:10

--- Stephen’s Accusation: Idols, the Temple, and the Indictment
kicker: II. Accusation
step: Idols | The same Moses who delivered them was refused again. While he was on the mountain, Israel turned back to Egypt’s idols and built a golden calf. [[Acts 7:35-43]]
step: The Temple | David wanted to build God a house but was denied because he was a man of war and had shed blood ([[1 Chronicles 28:3]]); his son Solomon was given the role instead. “The most High dwelleth not in temples made with hands”: the building was never the point, but a shadow of good things to come. [[Acts 7:44-50]]
fig: Stephen quotes Amos 5:25–27 | Amos 5:25-27 | mark: beyond Damascus
fig: Stephen quotes Isaiah 66:1–2 | Isaiah 66:1-2 | mark: where is the house that ye build unto me
step: The Indictment | “Ye stiffnecked... which of the prophets have not your fathers persecuted?” The same history Nehemiah confessed, Stephen turns into a charge. [[Acts 7:51-53]]

--- Saul, Consenting
kicker: II. Accusation
lead: The council is cut to the heart, not persuaded, but enraged. Stephen is dragged out of the city and stoned, praying for his killers as he dies.
scripture: Acts 7:58-60 | mark: whose name was Saul; Lord, lay not this sin to their charge
p: Standing in the crowd, at whose feet the witnesses laid down their clothes, is a young Pharisee named Saul, **consenting** to Stephen’s death. [[Acts 7:54-60|Acts 7:54–60]] [[Acts 8:1]]
take: Stephen is the first martyr, a powerful witness against unbelieving Israel. Saul will hear this testimony, and soon be the one giving it.

=== III. Repetition
--- Paul’s Repetition: The Second Witness
kicker: III. Repetition
scripture: 2 Corinthians 13:1 | mark: In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word be established
lead: Stephen is not the first to preach this against unbelieving Israel. Peter does, and Jesus certainly did in His own ministry. But Stephen’s witness, and his consenting unto death for the cause of Christ, was a powerful testimony to the truth, a truth Saul, now called Paul, would echo. Years later, in a synagogue at Pisidian Antioch, the very man who watched him die for it becomes the second, independently preaching the same history, to the same end: that it all points to Jesus.
scripture: Acts 13:17; Acts 13:22-23 | mark: raised unto Israel a Saviour, Jesus
p: Paul walks the synagogue through Egypt, the wilderness, the judges, and the kings, the very shape of the history Stephen preached, and lands it on the same name: Jesus. [[Acts 13:16-25]]

=== Interlude
--- A Rejection as Old as the Prophets
kicker: Interlude
lead: Before Stephen ever stood trial, Jesus leveled the same charge against the very city that would kill him, and named the pattern going all the way back to the beginning.
scripture: Matthew 23:29-31; Matthew 23:35-38 | mark: from the blood of righteous Abel
p: Jesus names **Abel** first, the very same Abel that Hebrews 11 is about to commend by faith. The line runs from the first martyr, to the prophets, to Jesus Himself. [[Matthew 23:29-39|Matthew 23:29–39]]

=== IV. Redemption
--- Hebrews’ Redemption: By Faith
kicker: IV. Redemption
lead: The same names are recited a fourth time, not as accusation, not as confession, but as a roll call of faith.
scripture: Hebrews 11:1-4 | mark: By faith Abel offered unto God a more excellent sacrifice than Cain
p: **Abel, Enoch, and Noah** obeyed God by faith before the Law ever existed, proof that faith, not ritual, has always been the standard. [[Hebrews 11:1-22|Hebrews 11:1–22]]
p: **Abraham and Sarah** believed a promise neither of them lived to see fully kept, and died still looking for “a city which hath foundations, whose builder and maker is God.” [[Hebrews 11:10]]

--- Hebrews’ Redemption: “Time Would Fail Me”
kicker: IV. Redemption
p: **Moses** refused the treasures of Egypt, choosing to suffer with God’s people rather than enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season. [[Hebrews 11:23-40|Hebrews 11:23–40]]
scripture: Hebrews 11:32; Hebrews 11:38-40 | mark: God having provided some better thing for us
take: Of whom the world was not worthy. Hebrews 11:38, the same men Stephen’s accusers claimed to honor.

=== The lesson
--- The Lesson
kicker: V
lead: Four settings, one history. What changes is not the facts, but the posture toward them.
tile: Nehemiah | Confession
tile: Stephen | Accusation
tile: Paul | Repetition
tile: Hebrews | Redemption
p: Saul stood by consenting when this history was preached as judgment against a stiffnecked people. Years later he stood up and preached it himself, and in his own epistles, came to hold up the very same men and women as redeemed by faith, a great cloud of witnesses.
take: The Old Testament story is both an indictment of men, who with the advantage of God’s grace and protection, the oracles of God, still fell short in their sin, but also a powerful example of His redemptive plan for man, through faith in His Word, calling upon the name of Jesus.
